"""Forty original computing ethics questions; the first choice is correct."""
RAW = '''
Computing ethics concerns|choices about technology and their effects on people|only processor clock speed|only the colour of a device|only the number of keys|Ethics concerns responsible choices and consequences of technology use.
Technical access to an account|does not by itself establish permission|always grants permission to publish its contents|removes every privacy obligation|changes the account into public data|Ability to access a system is different from authorisation.
Finding an unlocked account on a shared computer should prompt a user to|avoid private content and report the issue appropriately|copy all private files|change the owner's password|publish the messages|An unlocked account does not authorise reading or altering private content.
Before copying material, a responsible user should check|permission, licence conditions and relevant rules|only the size of the monitor|only the filename colour|only the keyboard layout|Permission and licence terms determine what uses may be allowed.
A free online image is|not automatically unrestricted for redistribution|always in the public domain|automatically owned by the viewer|a printer driver|Free access alone does not grant all reuse rights.
A licence describes|permitted uses and conditions|only a screen's size|only a user's handwriting|only hardware temperature|A licence states permissions and conditions for use.
Attribution means|acknowledging a source|automatically acquiring every right to the source|deleting the creator's name|converting the work into hardware|Attribution acknowledges a source.
Attribution alone|does not always grant permission where permission is required|always grants unlimited rights|eliminates every licence condition|makes every source anonymous|Giving credit does not replace permission where permission is required.
Plagiarism involves|presenting another person's work or ideas as one's own without appropriate acknowledgement|using a keyboard|storing a backup|testing an algorithm|Plagiarism concerns misrepresenting another person's work or ideas as one's own.
Copyright rules can protect|creative work and software under applicable law|only physical desks|only electricity cables|nothing on the internet|Copyright can protect creative works, including software, subject to applicable law.
The precise scope of copyright exceptions depends on|applicable law and circumstances|only the colour of the page|only the printer's age|whether a file has a short name|Rights and exceptions depend on the relevant legal context.
Privacy concerns include|appropriate handling of personal information|only printer ink|only screen resolution|only desk layout|Privacy includes how personal information is collected, used and shared.
A student photograph should be published only after considering|authorisation, context and possible harm|only its file size|only whether it is colourful|only whether a computer can open it|Responsible sharing requires considering permission, context and harm.
Removing a name from a dataset|does not automatically eliminate every re-identification risk|guarantees no person can ever be identified|makes all sharing lawful|turns data into hardware|Other information or combinations can still create identification risks.
For a public classroom demonstration, a safer general data choice is|fictional or appropriately authorised data|real passwords|private student records without permission|someone else's confidential messages|Fictional or authorised data can avoid unnecessary disclosure.
Accessibility aims to support|use by people with differing abilities|only faster network cables|only more colourful slides|the removal of all user testing|Accessibility helps people with different abilities use services and content.
Alternative text for meaningful images can help|some users access image information|increase physical RAM|replace all accessibility testing|fix electrical wiring|Appropriate alternative text can convey information in an image.
Keyboard navigation can support|accessibility for users who cannot use a pointing device|only printer speed|voltage regulation|disk repair|Keyboard access can help users navigate without a mouse.
Readable contrast can improve|access to visual content for some users|battery capacity|processor instruction count|network routing|Suitable contrast can make visual content easier to perceive.
No single accessibility feature|guarantees usability for every person|can help any user|should be tested|has any relation to design|Accessibility requires appropriate design and evaluation across different needs.
The digital divide concerns|unequal access to technology, connectivity or skills|only binary arithmetic|the difference between two printer models|only screen brightness|The digital divide includes differences in access and ability to use technology.
Which factor can contribute to a digital divide?|connectivity cost|a programme title alone|the existence of a folder|one variable name|Cost can limit access to digital services.
Providing devices alone may not eliminate the digital divide because|connectivity, electricity, skills and other barriers may remain|devices always solve every access problem|people never need training|all users have identical circumstances|Access barriers can include connectivity, power, language, disability and skills.
Online harassment can|harm individuals|never affect anyone|be treated as an input device|automatically become lawful when shared widely|Harassment can cause harm and should be addressed appropriately.
Before forwarding an alarming claim, a responsible user should|verify it using reliable sources|assume urgency proves truth|share it without reading|include another person's password|Verification reduces the risk of spreading inaccurate or harmful information.
An online message deleted from one account|may still exist in copies elsewhere|is guaranteed to disappear everywhere|cannot have been saved by anyone|automatically loses all privacy implications|Other recipients or systems may retain copies.
An accessible account belonging to another person should not be altered|without proper authorisation|unless it has a username|only when the screen is bright|only if it contains a document|Changing another person's account requires appropriate authorisation.
A public dataset may be reused|according to its applicable licence and conditions|without ever checking its terms|only by renaming it|only after removing all references|A dataset's licence and conditions govern permitted uses.
Open-source software licences|grant specified rights subject to their terms|always remove all conditions|guarantee the software is free of defects|automatically permit every use without conditions|Open-source licences grant defined rights but can impose conditions.
A contributor's work should be acknowledged|according to appropriate attribution and institutional rules|only if it was printed on paper|only if it has no author|never when used online|Attribution acknowledges the source and relevant contribution.
A user has permission to view a file. This does not necessarily permit|redistributing it to everyone|viewing it under the stated permission|following the licence|asking the owner for clarification|Permission can be limited to particular uses.
A user can technically change a database. A responsible user should|first ensure the change is authorised|assume technical ability creates permission|change records for curiosity|publish private entries|Technical capability does not confer authority to alter data.
Responsible AI use can include|testing quality, considering unequal effects and providing meaningful oversight|assuming confidence proves truth|ignoring affected users|publishing personal data without review|Safeguards depend on context and can include evaluation and oversight.
A route for a person to challenge an automated error can|support accountability|guarantee no errors occur|replace all testing|increase storage capacity|A challenge process can help address incorrect automated decisions.
Using a public-domain work still calls for|checking the applicable status and any relevant attribution rules|assuming every online file is public domain|removing all source information|ignoring institutional policies|Status and academic attribution obligations should be checked in context.
A person shares another student's password without authorisation. This is|an improper disclosure of sensitive information|a normal backup|an accessibility feature|a flowchart decision|Passwords should be protected and not shared without authorisation.
Which question is useful before sharing a claim?|is it accurate and am I authorised to share it?|is the file icon blue?|does the message contain a long filename?|is the sender's computer large?|Accuracy and authorisation matter before sharing.
A technology's social effects|depend on its design, deployment and context|are always positive|are always negative|never affect people|Technology can bring benefits and harms depending on context.
A student quotes a source in an assignment. They should|follow the applicable citation and permission requirements|assume quotation needs no acknowledgement|claim the words are their own|delete source information|Citation and permission requirements depend on the use and institutional rules.
When uncertain about a sharing request, a responsible user should|pause and seek appropriate guidance|publish immediately|share all related passwords|assume silence grants permission|Seeking guidance can prevent unauthorised or harmful sharing.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
